===========================================================================
Grading module: control point detection (2025 fix)
===========================================================================

Bug
---
Pictures of severely (perspectively) deformed exams were rejected: the
grading module could not find the 21 control dots and answered "Nao
encontrei os pontos de controle com as restricoes dadas!".

Cause
-----
The four outer dots were searched with a combinatorial O(n^4) enumeration
of the blobs closest to the four corners of the picture, accepting only
quadrilaterals that were almost *rectangles*: corners of 90 degrees within
"Angle Tolerance", a prescribed "Correct Side Ratio" and a parallelogram
test ("Target Radius"). None of these is invariant under a projective
transformation, so a picture taken from an oblique point of view (where
the outer quadrilateral has no right angle, no parallel sides and no fixed
side ratio) never satisfied them.

Fix (mixnfix/ControlPointDetector.java)
---------------------------------------
The rectangle hypotheses were replaced by a projective search:

  1. every valid blob is a dot candidate (no corner-of-the-picture bias);
  2. the extreme points of the cloud are computed with a convex hull
     (a second hull layer is peeled when needed): under any perspective
     the four outer dots are extreme points;
  3. quadrilaterals of extreme points are enumerated by decreasing area
     and each one (4 rotations) defines a homography from the theoretical
     sheet to the picture;
  4. a hypothesis is scored by mapping *all* 21 theoretical control points
     and counting how many fall on a detected blob - a verification that
     is invariant under perspective;
  5. a local RANSAC over the matched dots plus a least squares homography
     refinement give the final, sub pixel accurate mapping; missing dots
     (too faint at that threshold) are predicted by the homography, which
     is exact for a planar target.

Efficiency
----------
  * connected components are now computed for one threshold at a time with
    a union-find over two scan lines (the old code kept a static
    8 x 1000 x 1000 matrix of region references, ~64 MB, and always
    labelled the image for the 8 thresholds);
  * the search stops at the first threshold that yields a complete and
    accurate grid;
  * nearest blob queries use a uniform spatial hash grid, hypotheses are
    bounded and pruned by area with a branch and bound cut on the score.
  * pictures are no longer limited to 1000x1000 pixels.

Typical cost on the sample exams (423x517 and 425x550): about 30-60 ms of
cpu time per exam for the whole "Corrigir" step.

Verification output
-------------------
Every graded frame is exported to $mixnfix.output.dir (default /app/output)
as <picture>.jpg: the grey scale (black and white) exam frame with the
detected control points painted in yellow.

  ./scripts/run_grading_test.sh       # composed images, GUI images, cpu times
  ./scripts/run_robustness_test.sh    # warps, enlarged pictures, regression

run_grading_test.sh calls, for the weakly deformed (img2.jpg) and for the
severely deformed (img1.jpg) exam, the routines associated with the
"Corrigir" and "ID & Code" buttons of the grading GUI and writes:

  /app/output/img1.jpg, img2.jpg, composed_result.jpg
  /app/output/gui_img1_corrigir.jpg, gui_img1_id_code.jpg
  /app/output/gui_img2_corrigir.jpg, gui_img2_id_code.jpg
  /app/output/grading_cpu_times.txt

===========================================================================
New question type: Subjetiva_9 (2026 addition)
===========================================================================

A new question type was added, "Subjetiva_9", analogous to the existing
"Subjetiva_5" but with 9 gradable levels instead of 5 (the teacher marks one
of 0, 1/8, 2/8, ..., 7/8, 1 of the question's value, instead of one of
0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1).

Every place that depended on the list of question types was updated:

  * mixnfix/prova/Quesito.java
      - Quesito.TIPO_SUBJETIVA_9 constant, getTipo()/setTipo() persistence
        ("subjetiva_9" property value), detalharNota()/avaliarNota() (the
        marking -> grade conversion, dividing by 8 instead of 4),
        getTextRespostaAluno()/getTextRespostaCorreta() (kept as a no-op,
        same as Subjetiva_5: there is no predefined "correct answer", the
        teacher grades by hand).
  * mixnfix/gui/PanelCriarQuesito.java, mixnfix/gui/MFActions.java
      - "Tipo Quesito" combo box (ID_SUBJETIVO_9/SUBJETIVO_9) in the
        "Adicionar Quesito"/"Alterar valores da questão" dialogs.
  * mixnfix/folharesposta/FolhaRespostaQuestionarioBasico.java,
    mixnfix/folharesposta/GeradorFolhaRespostas.java
      - the response-sheet builders instantiate FRSubjetiva(numQuesito, 9)
        (the FRSubjetiva inner class already took the number of markable
        items as a parameter, so it needed no further change besides being
        called with 9 instead of 5).
  * mixnfix/gui/QuestionarioUI.java, mixnfix/gui/CorrecaoUI.java,
    mixnfix/gui/ModelEntradaColetaQuestionario.java,
    mixnfix/gui/ModelEntradaProvaCorrecao.java
      - wherever TIPO_ALTERNATIVAS/TIPO_SUBJETIVA_5 were handled together
        (single-choice OptionField, one out of N cells), TIPO_SUBJETIVA_9
        was added to the same code path (mixnfix/folharesposta/OptionField
        itself is already generic on the number of cells, so no change was
        needed there).

Validation / screenshots
-------------------------
tools/DemoSubjetiva9.java (+ tools/ExamPdfFallback.java) is a headless
driver, in the same spirit as tools/GradingGUIRun.java, that exercises the
*real* Swing panels (PanelCriarQuesito, PanelModelProva, PanelProva,
PanelCalibragemCorrecao, MFTree/MFTreeModel, ...) end-to-end:

  1. creates a folder ("Adicionar Pasta") and imports AVLC-2-2025-EE3.prova
     ("Importar Prova");
  2. opens "Adicionar Quesito" over "Cabeçalho", forces the "Tipo Quesito"
     combo box's drop down open (all 5 types, including the new
     "subjetiva_9") and renders the whole composite UI;
  3. picks "subjetiva_9", adds the question to the exam and renders the UI
     with the new question selected in the central panel;
  4. produces the exam's PDF ("Produzir Prova" / "Produzir") and rasterizes
     it to an image;
  5. adds a "Correção" ("Adicionar Correção"), opens its calibration panel,
     loads the PDF produced in the previous step via "Foto", and renders a
     snapshot of the whole UI.

Because this sandbox has neither a display server nor pdflatex/epstopdf
(and no network access to install a LaTeX toolchain), two adaptations were
made, both documented at the top of tools/DemoSubjetiva9.java and
tools/ExamPdfFallback.java: (a) every panel/dialog/popup is rendered off
screen with Component.printAll(), never Window.setVisible(); (b) the exam
PDF is produced by a small, self-contained, valid PDF writer instead of
pdflatex, listing the exam header and every question's text (extracted
through the real mixnfix.prova.PanelPapel component).

Usage:

  ./build.sh
  ./initdb.sh
  javac -encoding UTF-8 -cp "bin:lib/derby.jar:lib/jxl.jar" -sourcepath src \
        -d bin tools/DemoSubjetiva9.java tools/ExamPdfFallback.java
  cd src && java -Djava.awt.headless=true \
        --add-opens java.desktop/javax.swing.plaf.basic=ALL-UNNAMED \
        -cp "../bin:../lib/derby.jar:../lib/jxl.jar" mixnfix.gui.DemoSubjetiva9

Output (in /app/output by default):

  subjetiva9_step1_tipo_quesito_dropdown.jpg
  subjetiva9_step2_question_added_and_selected.jpg
  subjetiva9_step3_pdf_render.jpg
  subjetiva9_step4_correcao_calibragem.jpg
  subjetiva9_exam.pdf, subjetiva9_exam_source.txt
